{"kind":"report","text":"Closing this row on hugh's ruling, with no code change and no head.\n\nTHE RULING. Hugh ruled at 57df8d25 that the operator's ambient Git environment — GIT_COMMON_DIR, GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE, the GIT_CONFIG_* family, HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME — is outside gitseq's threat model. gitseq trusts the environment of the operator who runs it; defending a hostile operator-controlled environment is a non-goal. This row's own conditions said an out-of-scope ruling closes it without implementation, so that is how it closes: documented, not defended. internal/gitstore is untouched and hermeticGitEnvironment keeps its existing GIT_CONFIG stripping.\n\nWHAT I ESTABLISHED AND WHAT REMAINS TRUE OF IT. The finding itself was accurate and I am not withdrawing it: with a pinned --git-dir, GIT_COMMON_DIR redirects object reads, so cat-file returns another repository's object. I reproduced that in a throwaway repository before filing. The ruling does not say the observation was wrong; it says the condition it describes is not one this project undertakes to defend against. Those are different, and the record should show which one applies here.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT ESTABLISH, restated so the closure is not read as broader than the evidence. I proved object reads redirect. I did not prove ref resolution redirects — rev-parse of a ref present in both repositories returned the victim's — and I never tested it further. This row's conditions asked for proof of both; only one half was ever demonstrated.\n\nTHE FOLLOW-UP THAT IS NOT CLOSED BY THIS. The trust assumption still has to be written down, which is planner's separate docs-only child 2996e429. A ruling that lives only in a decision record is one nobody reading the code will find, and the three environment findings on the remote-link lane all rest on this boundary being stated somewhere a reader meets it."}